I want build an application that adds visual information while I'm playwing Warhammer 40k: Kill Team. Kill Team is a boardgame that is played on a board, with terrain elements on top and plastic soilders (operatives). I will place a camera above the board in a fixed position, connect it to computer and I want to see additional information overlayed on the original video feed in augmented reality fashion.

I want to make measurements on the board. In order to do this, I need to indentify the corners of the board. I have been trying computer vision algorithms with mixed results. I'm perfectly fine with putting QR/ArUco/AprilTags next to the corners of the board to identify them better. The app needs to correct for various camera distorsions, the goal is to have a flat, even, correctly rotated image with right angles. If there needs to be calibration step, it is fine. The app should be tolerant to the accidental movement of the board, it is fine if recalibration is needed in such cases.

The game measures in inches. One of the features would be to add a grid overlay with 1 inch spacing. This would help placing the terrain on the board during game setup. We have pictograms showing various terrain placement configurations (see image).

Identifying operatives on the board would be the next big task. Each operative is on a circular base with various diameters (25mm, 28mm, 32mm, 40mm, 50mm). All measurements between operatives is a measurement between bases. Due to the terrain pieces, some of the base silhuette might be visually blocked. There could be several circular tokens on the board to which we should measure distance to. Some of the tokens might have an "aura" that takes effect in a certain distance. Most tokens have a 1" control range that we have to be aware of.

The next big task would be to tell apart operatives, so we can tell which one is which and we can track various attributes (mainly "wounds", which is the health) and statuses (poision, injured, etc.) on each. It would be nice if the computer vision alone could tell apart the operatives. Providing 10-20 images of the operatives and training on them is fine. What I want to avoid is having to put QR codes on the operative models. I'm really counting on you giving me options on how this could be done in a way that works in practive. Give me multiple options.

Although the operatives move on the flat board, some maps have terrain with "Vantage" elements where operative can climb on top. These Vantage parts are flat and parallel to the ground, on a certain height (3" and 6" from the ground). Supporting the positioning of operatives on vantages will not be part of the MVP, but it would be nice to support it later on.